/**
 * Returns a cached or newly created {@code FastDateFormat} instance that formats
 * both date and time using the specified styles, time zone, and locale.
 * This method provides a fast and thread-safe way to obtain a date/time formatter.
 *
 * The method first attempts to retrieve an existing {@code FastDateFormat} instance
 * from an internal cache based on the provided {@code dateStyle}, {@code timeStyle},
 * {@code timeZone}, and {@code locale}. If a matching instance is found, it is
 * returned directly, improving performance by avoiding redundant object creation.
 *
 * If no cached instance is available, a new {@code FastDateFormat} is constructed.
 * This involves:
 * 1. Determining the effective {@code Locale} (defaults to {@code Locale.getDefault()} if null).
 * 2. Determining the effective {@code TimeZone} (defaults to {@code TimeZone.getDefault()} if null).
 * 3. Obtaining a standard {@code SimpleDateFormat} instance from {@code DateFormat.getDateTimeInstance}
 *    using the specified styles and effective locale.
 * 4. Extracting the pattern string from the {@code SimpleDateFormat}.
 * 5. Creating a new {@code FastDateFormat} instance using the extracted pattern,
 *    effective time zone, and effective locale.
 * 6. Caching this newly created instance for future requests.
 *
 * This method is synchronized to ensure thread safety when accessing and modifying the cache.
 *
 * @param dateStyle The style of the date formatting (e.g., {@code DateFormat.FULL}, {@code DateFormat.LONG},
 *                  {@code DateFormat.MEDIUM}, {@code DateFormat.SHORT}).
